            "result_text": "Done! I've created `/app/run.py` with the `run_tasks` function.\n\nHere's how it works:\n\n1. **Concurrency control**: Uses `asyncio.Semaphore` to limit how many tasks run simultaneously—each task acquires the semaphore before running\n2. **Keyboard interrupt handling**: When you hit Ctrl+C:\n   - All pending tasks are cancelled (sending them `CancelledError`)\n   - The code waits for all tasks to finish via `asyncio.gather(..., return_exceptions=True)` so cancellation completes\n   - Then re-raises the `KeyboardInterrupt`\n3. **Cleanup guarantees**: Any `try/finally` blocks or `async with` context managers in your tasks will still execute, even after cancellation\n\nYou can use it like this:\n\n```python\nfrom run import run_tasks\nimport asyncio\n\nasync def my_task():\n    try:\n        await asyncio.sleep(10)\n    finally:\n        print(\"Cleanup!\")\n\nasyncio.run(run_tasks([my_task, my_task], max_concurrent=2))\n```"
